    "q-346050": {
      "dom_id": "step-q346050",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "A surveyor stands $15\\text{ ft}$ away from the base of a radio tower. An elevator inside the tower moves straight up at a constant speed of $20\\text{ ft/s}.$ How fast is the distance between the surveyor and the elevator changing when the elevator is $20\\text{ ft}$ above the ground?\n\nThe distance between the surveyor and the elevator is\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft/s}.$",
      "worked_solution": "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14208490/assets/7a6f5b05bc0bd89d01b1b521fa35f4bc613e532a06ce04fea26323e6c5dbbafa.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between the surveyor and the base of the tower,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the elevator and the ground,\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the surveyor and the elevator.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $z,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=15\\,\\text{ft}$ and $y=20\\,\\text{ft}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {15}^{2}+{20}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 625 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ z & =25\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=20\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s},$\n\n- the distance between the surveyor and the base of the tower remains the same, so $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=0.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(15)(0)+2(20)(20) & =2(25)\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 800 & =50\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between the surveyor and the elevator is $16\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
