      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14208490/observations/step-q346050-9c81b6fdce11004984d2.html",
      "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14208490/observations/step-q346050-9c81b6fdce11004984d2.png",
      "source_url": "https://mathacademy.com/tasks/14208490/topics/345/lesson",
      "captured_at": 1791995361.9755466,
      "math_academy_id": "q-346050",
      "topic_id": "345",
      "sequence_position": null,
      "source_step": {
        "dom_id": "step-q346050",
        "placement_id": null,
        "content_id": null,
        "type": null
      },
      "source_example_id": "e-229",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Hypotenuse Using the Pythagorean Theorem",
      "kp_id": "e-229"
    },
    "q-104079": {
      "dom_id": "step-q104079",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14208490/assets/9dbee02f0c07041dd8424faf683952ada2f7f1a604f9bf89a65588eaf3b6f9f6.png)\n\nThe legs of an isosceles right triangle are increasing. The length, measured in feet, of each leg is given by $x(t)=t+{t}^{2},$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. What is the rate of the change of the length of the hypotenuse after $2$ seconds? Give your answer rounded to $1$ decimal place.",
      "worked_solution": "In the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the length of the equal sides, and\n\n- $z$ is the length of the hypotenuse.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $x,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $z,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the length of the equal sides after $2$ seconds. We get \n\n$$\nx=x(2)=(2)+(2)^{2}=6\\text{ ft}.\n$$\n\nWe can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{x}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {6}^{2}+{6}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 72 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ z & ≈8.485\\,3\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rate of change of the sides is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={x}^{′}(t)=2t+1.$ So, when $t=2$ their velocity is\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={x}^{′}(2)=2(2)+1=5\\text{ ft/s}.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(6)(5)+2(6)(5) & ≈2(8.485\\,3)\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & ≈7.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the hypotenuse's length is changing at a rate of $7.1\\text{ ft/s}$ after $2$ seconds.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
