      "source_example_id": "e-229",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Hypotenuse Using the Pythagorean Theorem",
      "kp_id": "e-229"
    },
    "q-2942": {
      "dom_id": "step-q2942",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "The length of one leg of a right triangle increases at the rate of $3\\text{ m/s},$ while the length of the second leg is decreasing at the rate of $2\\text{ m/s}.$ How fast is the length of the hypotenuse changing when the length of the increasing leg is $12\\text{ m}$ and the length of the decreasing leg is $16\\text{ m}?$",
      "worked_solution": "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14208490/assets/eee3de464da57671142e2677974b44c63dc2397215ffdd2aa36e2c3d401a647d.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the length of the increasing side,\n\n- $y$ is the length of the decreasing side,\n\n- $z$ is the length of the hypotenuse.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $z,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=12\\,\\text{m}$ and $y=16\\,\\text{m}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {12}^{2}+{16}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 400 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ z & =20\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=3\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $x=12\\,\\text{m}$ and $y=16\\,\\text{m},$\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-2\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$  when $x=12\\,\\text{m}$ and $y=16\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(12)(3)+2(16)(-2) & =2(20)\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 8 & =40\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{8}{40} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the hypotenuse is changing at a rate of $\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
