    },
    "q-48953": {
      "dom_id": "step-q48953",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Two buses, $A$ and $B,$ drive toward a school. Bus $A$ travels south at a constant velocity of $30\\,\\text{ mph},$ while the bus $B$ travels west at a constant velocity of $40\\,\\text{mph}.$ If bus $A$ is $16$ miles from the school, and bus $B$ is $12$ miles from the school, how fast is the distance between the buses decreasing?",
      "worked_solution": "Let's draw a diagram of the situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14208490/assets/07eb0e89a4dc85160deb45ee13e6189d32bbc905bc638cb1aee891f0504ca950.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between bus $A$ and school,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between bus $B$ and school,\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the two buses.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $z,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=12\\,\\text{mi}$ and $y=16\\,\\text{mi}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =(12)^{2}+(16)^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =400 \\\\ z & =20\\,\\text{mi}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-40\\,\\text{mph},$\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-30\\,\\text{mph}.$\n\nNote that the rates of change are both negative because the distance to the school is decreasing for both buses. \n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(12)(-40)+2(16)(-30) & =2(20)\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 40\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =-960-960 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1920}{40} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =-48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between the buses is decreasing at a rate of $48\\,\\text{mph}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
