    "q-104077": {
      "dom_id": "step-q104077",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "After attending school, Elizabeth and Jessica leave at the same time. Elizabeth heads north at a speed of $4.5\\text{ mph},$ while Jessica heads east at a speed of $6\\text{ mph}.$ How fast are the two moving away from each other after walking for $2$ hours?",
      "worked_solution": "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14208490/assets/2b012a853cf973cc68df35bbd94a498fd1d0d907548c1f6d8ca3f842c967c877.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between Jessica and the school,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between Elizabeth and the school, and\n\n- $z$ is the distance between Elizabeth and Jessica.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $z,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nFirst, we determine Elizabeth's distance from school and Jessica's distance from school after $2$ hours. Since their velocities are constants, we get \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot t=4.5(2)=9\\text{ mi}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot t=6(2)=12\\text{ mi}.\n$$\n\nWe can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {9}^{2}+{12}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 225 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ z & =15\\,\\text{mi}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=4.5\\,\\text{mph}$ after $2$ hours,\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=6\\,\\text{mph}$ after $2$ hours.\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(9)(4.5)+2(12)(6) & =2(15)\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 225 & =30\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =7.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between Elizabeth and Jessica increases at a rate of $7.5\\,\\text{mph}$ after walking $2$ hours.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-104077-a",
